Electrical discharge machining of Hastelloy has been studied with a focus on graphite tool electrodes; these studies report clear links between electrode material properties, machining parameters, and performance metrics such as material removal rate (MRR), tool wear rate (TWR), and surface roughness (Ra). Nowicki and co‑workers investigated POCO graphite electrodes with grain sizes of 1 µm (AF‑5) and 10 µm (S‑180) on Hastelloy C‑22 and found that larger‑grained graphite gives higher process efficiency (higher MRR) but also higher relative tool wear, while smaller‑grained graphite yields the lowest surface roughness. {{cite:S1#c1}}  

A related study by Nowicki et al. frames the grain‑size issue as an industrial challenge and reports that fine‑grained graphite increases process efficiency and reduces tool wear; the authors also note that detached graphite grains affect discharge stability and process efficiency, and they provide empirical and mathematical models linking grain size and machining parameters to MRR, relative TWR, and Ra for negative polarity EDM of Hastelloy C‑22. {{cite:S3#c1}}  

These two experimental reports lead to a tension in the literature: one set of results associates larger graphite grains with higher MRR (at the cost of greater relative tool wear), while the other emphasizes improved efficiency and reduced wear with fine grains. Both studies, however, agree that discharge current and pulse duration are primary factors controlling MRR and Ra; additionally, one study identifies the time interval (pulse‑off time) as the dominant parameter governing relative wear of the graphite electrode. {{cite:S1#c1}}{{cite:S3#c1}}  

Broader EDM literature on difficult‑to‑machine alloys reinforces the dominance of electrical parameters: Taguchi and ANOVA analyses in cryogenically treated superalloys and in titanium workpieces identify peak current (amperage) and pulse‑on time as the most influential factors for MRR, Ra and TWR. {{cite:S2#c1}}{{cite:S8#c1}} Ramabalan et al. specifically link current and pulse duration to pronounced electrode shape changes and wear when machining Hastelloy B2, reporting that unstable sparks and heat diffusion transform sharp electrode features into hemispherical ends. {{cite:S4#c1}}  

The EDM field recognises persistent challenges—high TWR, low MRR and elevated surface roughness—and reports multiple mitigation approaches such as cryogenic treatment, powder‑mixed dielectrics, ultrasonic assistance and coating methods; these association techniques are evaluated with respect to MRR, TWR, surface integrity and recast layer formation. {{cite:S6#c1}}{{cite:S7#c1}}{{cite:S5#c1}}  

