rows.bRaw:
Graphite electrodes dominate recent investigations of EDM on nickel-based superalloys because they combine low raw-material cost, machinability, and high sublimation temperature, properties that directly influence process efficiency, accuracy and surface finish. {{cite:S1#c1}} Experimental comparisons using POCO graphite grades with grain sizes of 1 µm (AF-5) and 10 µm (S-180/EDM-180) report that finer-grained graphite reduces surface roughness while coarser grains increase material removal rate but also raise relative tool wear. {{cite:S1#c1}} {{cite:S3#c1}}

Multiple studies identify discharge current (peak current) and pulse-on time (pulse duration) as the principal electrical parameters that govern material removal rate and surface roughness in EDM. {{cite:S1#c1}} {{cite:S2#c1}} Specifically, Taguchi and ANOVA analyses in a factorial design found peak current to explain the largest share of variation in both Ra and MRR in the tested superalloy samples. {{cite:S2#c1}} Other work similarly highlights current and pulse duration as dominant influences on electrode wear and the transformation of electrode geometry under unstable sparking conditions. {{cite:S4#c1}}

Mechanistically, studies link graphite grain size to discharge stability and removal efficiency: fine grains improve process stability and lower tool wear, whereas coarse grains detach and perturb the discharge, increasing both MRR and relative electrode depletion. {{cite:S3#c1}} The experimental research on Hastelloy C-22 produced empirical and mathematical models that predict how machining parameters and graphite grain size interact to determine MRR, relative tool wear rate (TWR), and Ra. {{cite:S1#c1}} {{cite:S3#c1}}

Attempts to mitigate the key EDM limitations—high TWR, low MRR, and poor surface integrity—span several auxiliary methods, including cryogenic treatments, powder-mixed dielectrics, ultrasonic assistance, and hybrid process variants; reviews identify these approaches and note gaps where process physics and parametric sensitivity require further experimental and modelling work. {{cite:S6#c1}} {{cite:S7#c1}} In cryogenically treated workpieces, optimization studies using Taguchi-based methods report that combinations of cryogenic state, pulse-on time and peak current yield the best trade-offs for Ra and MRR under the examined conditions. {{cite:S2#c1}}

Despite detailed results for graphite electrodes on Hastelloy grades, the literature pins several gaps relevant to this thesis scope. Empirical evidence for copper electrodes, and for engineered composite electrodes comparing TWR and MRR directly against the graphite cases on Hastelloy, is not present in the supplied sources. [[NEEDS SOURCE: comparative data for copper and composite electrodes]]

rows.aRaw:
Hastelloy C-22 and similar nickel-based superalloys are commonly processed by EDM when conventional machining is impractical, and the choice of tool electrode material strongly affects process efficiency, accuracy, and surface integrity. {{cite:S1#c1}} Studies that specifically evaluate graphite electrodes on Hastelloy C-22 report that graphite grain size alters material removal rate (MRR), tool wear rate (TWR), and surface roughness (Ra): larger-grained graphite gives higher process efficiency but higher relative tool wear, while fine-grained graphite produces lower Ra. {{cite:S1#c1}} A later investigation using POCO AF-5 (1 µm) and POCO EDM-180 (10 µm) corroborates that fine-grained graphite increases process efficiency and reduces tool wear, and notes that detached graphite grains influence discharge stability and overall process effectiveness. {{cite:S3#c1}}

Multiple studies identify the same primary EDM parameters as dominant drivers of MRR and surface finish. For Hastelloy work, discharge current and pulse duration are reported as the main factors controlling MRR and Ra for both fine- and coarse-grained graphite electrodes, while the time interval (pulse-off time) primarily governs relative electrode wear for graphite. {{cite:S1#c1}} In other EDM investigations across different superalloys and treatments, peak current emerged as the most influential factor on both MRR and Ra, explaining large proportions of variance in ANOVA analyses. {{cite:S2#c1}} Likewise, work on titanium and other alloys finds amperage (current) overwhelmingly determines MRR and TWR in experimental designs using Taguchi and regression analyses. {{cite:S8#c1}}

Electrode geometry and thermal effects further determine wear patterns and dimensional accuracy during EDM of nickel alloys. Experiments on Hastelloy B2 show that current and pulse duration significantly affect electrode taper, radius, and wear length, with sharp electrode edges transforming to hemispherical shapes due to unstable sparks and heat diffusion. {{cite:S4#c1}} More generally, reviews of EDM identify persistent challenges of high TWR, low MRR, and rough surfaces, and document multiple mitigation strategies—cryogenic treatment, powder-mixed dielectrics, ultrasonic assistance, and combinations thereof—to improve MRR, TWR, and surface integrity. {{cite:S6#c1}} Experimental work adding nanopowder to the dielectric and applying cryogenic treatment to electrodes reports substantial reductions in electrode wear and overcut in some configurations, illustrating that electrode material condition and dielectric composition can together shift EDM performance. {{cite:S7#c1}}

Although these studies define how graphite grain size and process variables affect EDM performance on Hastelloy, direct experimental comparisons between composite electrodes and conventional (copper or graphite) electrodes for Hastelloy are not presented in the cited literature. [[NEEDS SOURCE: composite vs conventional electrodes comparison for Hastelloy]]

rows.bRaw:
Electrical discharge machining (EDM) is widely applied to nickel-based alloys that are difficult to machine by conventional methods, and the physical and mechanical properties of the electrode material strongly affect machining efficiency, accuracy and surface finish. {{cite:S1#c1}} The principal process limitations reported across reviews and studies are elevated tool wear rate, limited material removal rate and poor surface integrity caused by the repetitive electrical discharges between tool and workpiece. {{cite:S6#c1}} 

Several studies focused specifically on graphite electrodes for Hastelloy C‑22 and report that graphite microstructure strongly influences MRR, relative tool wear rate (TWR) and surface roughness (Ra). {{cite:S1#c1}} Experiments using POCO graphites with grain sizes of 1 µm (AF‑5) and 10 µm (S‑180) found that coarser-grained graphite gives higher process efficiency but also larger relative tool wear, whereas finer-grained graphite produces lower Ra. {{cite:S1#c1}} Follow-up work that used similar materials and negative polarity confirmed that fine-grained graphite improves efficiency and reduces tool wear, and additionally observed that graphite grains detaching from the electrode influence spark stability and process effectiveness. {{cite:S3#c1}} Mathematical models derived from these experiments were proposed to predict machining outcomes under different parameter sets. {{cite:S3#c1}}

Parametric studies across different alloys highlight the dominant role of electrical parameters on MRR, TWR and surface finish. {{cite:S2#c1}} For example, Taguchi and ANOVA analyses indicate peak current and pulse-on time as primary factors controlling MRR and Ra in EDM tests; one report attributes peak current to over 74% influence on Ra and over 86% on MRR in their design. {{cite:S2#c1}} Other investigations using design‑of‑experiments and ANOVA identify amperage (current) as the most significant factor for MRR and TWR, sometimes exceeding 99% contribution in their models. {{cite:S8#c1}} Work on Hastelloy B2 also shows that current and pulse duration markedly affect electrode shape, wear length and cutting time, and that unstable sparks can transform sharp electrode edges into hemispherical ends due to heat diffusion. {{cite:S4#c1}}

Attempts to improve EDM performance by modifying the process environment or electrode treatments are reported in the literature: cryogenic treatments, powder-mixed dielectrics, ultrasonic assistance and hybrid methods have been tested to reduce TWR, increase MRR and improve dimensional accuracy. {{cite:S6#c1}} Studies using nanopowder in the dielectric and cryogenically treated brass electrodes demonstrate substantial reductions in electrode wear rate and overcut in their experiments, with optimization performed via ANNs and multi-objective algorithms. {{cite:S7#c1}} Separately, electrical discharge coating (EDC) is documented as a route to alter surface properties via discharge-mediated deposition, suggesting opportunities to influence surface integrity after EDM. {{cite:S5#c1}}

