Selective Laser Melting (SLM) builds metal parts by selectively melting and fusing powder layers with a high-power laser, producing near–full-density components suitable for functional testing and comparison with conventionally made materials. {{cite:S1#c1}}

Studies of SLM-produced 18Ni-300 (grade 300) maraging steel report an as-built microstructure that is very fine and martensitic, with submicron grains consistent with extremely high cooling rates. {{cite:S3#c1}} The as-printed condition is described as a fine-grain martensitic structure when examined in multiple section orientations, with no strong preferred texture or anisotropy reported in one study. {{cite:S8#c1}} Reviews of AM steels attribute enhanced strength, wear resistance and corrosion resistance of some AM steels to unique microstructural features such as hierarchical subgrains and fine precipitates formed during the process. {{cite:S6#c1}}

Heat treatments modify these as-built features through precipitation, residual-stress relief, and phase transformations. {{cite:S3#c1}} One experimental comparison shows that thermochemical treatment (plasma nitriding) performed after solution treatment and/or aging alters the morphology and chemistry of surface layers, with prior heat treatment influencing the resulting nitrided layer and subsequent wear and corrosion performance. {{cite:S2#c1}} Aging of SLM maraging steel is reported to produce maximum nano-hardness and to be the principal factor controlling both hardness and corrosion potential in a direct comparison with wrought counterparts. {{cite:S8#c1}}

Several studies specifically examine reversed austenite formed by intercritical heat treatments and its influence on pitting in chloride-containing electrolytes. {{cite:S4#c1}} An intercritical tempering within the mixed α′ + γ range at 720 °C produced reversed austenite that precipitated primarily along martensite lath boundaries, attributed to local Ni enrichment from dissolution of intermetallic particles. {{cite:S4#c1}} Potentiodynamic polarization and open-circuit potential tests in 3.5 wt% NaCl indicate that the specimen tempered at 720 °C shows a higher pitting potential, lower corrosion current density, and lower corrosion rate than as-printed, conventionally aged, and homogenized specimens without reversed austenite, suggesting reversed austenite improves pitting resistance in that study. {{cite:S4#c1}}

Surface condition and post-processing also affect corrosion behaviour: electropolishing reduced surface roughness substantially and studies comparing SLM and wrought maraging steel report similar nano-hardness and corrosion behaviour after heat treatment, with precipitation identified as the main factor influencing both properties. {{cite:S8#c1}} Thermochemical surface treatments such as nitriding interact with prior heat treatments to influence crack formation in compound layers and thereby affect corrosion and wear outcomes. {{cite:S2#c1}}

rows.bRaw:
### 

Selective Laser Melting (SLM) produces near-full-density metal parts by layerwise melting and fusion of powder with a high-power laser, a process that enables fabrication of complex geometries and has been widely applied to steels including maraging grades. {{cite:S1#c1}}  

As-built microstructures of SLM maraging steel are characteristically very fine and martensitic. One study reports submicron prior-austenite or martensitic features with an average size of about $0.31\ \mu$m, implying cooling rates on the order of $10^{7}\ \mathrm{K/s}$, and finds dense populations of needle-shaped nanoprecipitates of $ \mathrm{Ni_3X}$ (X = Ti, Al, Mo) within a martensitic matrix that account for age-hardening. {{cite:S3#c1}} Another microstructural survey observed a fine-grained martensitic structure in as-printed maraging steel across multiple section orientations and detected no pronounced texture or anisotropy in the as-built microstructure. {{cite:S8#c1}}  

The thermal history imposed by SLM acts as an intrinsic heat treatment: repeated thermal cycles during building drive in situ tempering, precipitation and other phase changes that distinguish AM microstructures from conventionally produced material. {{cite:S6#c1}} Subsequent deliberate heat treatments modify these as-built features: systematic aging increases nano-hardness and maximizes precipitation strengthening, while solution treatments and aging can influence residual stress, anisotropy and precipitate morphology. {{cite:S3#c1}}{{cite:S8#c1}} Combining thermochemical treatments with conventional heat treatments also alters surface compound layers and near-surface chemistry—plasma nitriding performed at $520\ ^\circ\mathrm{C}$ for 6 h was studied both alone and following solution/aging steps to probe wear and corrosion responses. {{cite:S2#c1}}  

Reversion of martensite to austenite during intercritical tempering has been linked to both microstructural redistribution of alloying elements and electrochemical performance. One study tempered SLM maraging steel at an intercritical temperature ($720\ ^\circ\mathrm{C}$) and found that reversed austenite precipitated primarily along martensite-lath boundaries where local Ni enrichment from dissolved intermetallics promoted nucleation; the specimen with reversed austenite displayed a higher pitting potential, lower corrosion current density and a reduced corrosion rate in 3.5 wt% NaCl compared with as-printed, aged and homogenized counterparts. {{cite:S4#c1}}  

Surface state and near-surface microstructure also affect corrosion. Electropolishing reduced SLM maraging steel surface roughness by about 65%, and studies report that despite finer microstructure the corrosion behaviour of heat-treated SLM parts can be similar to that of conventionally wrought material, with precipitation state strongly influencing both hardness and electrochemical potential. {{cite:S8#c1}}{{cite:S3#c1}}  

Nitriding without prior aging produced defects in the compound nitride layer, whereas applying nitriding after prior heat treatments improved wear and corrosion resistance, indicating that prior microstructural state governs the success of thermochemical surface treatments. {{cite:S2#c1}}

rows.aRaw:
Selective laser melting (SLM) produces near-full-density metallic parts by layerwise melting and fusion of powder with a high-power laser, a process capable of yielding parts up to 99.9% relative density. {{cite:S1#c1}}

As-built SLM maraging steel commonly exhibits a very fine martensitic microstructure with extremely small submicron grains and a high density of nanoscale precipitates produced by the rapid solidification inherent to the process. {{cite:S3#c1}} Electron microscopy studies report submicron grain sizes (0.31 μm average) and massive needle-shaped Ni3X (X = Ti, Al, Mo) nanoprecipitates embedded in a martensitic matrix in SLM-produced grade 300 maraging steel, consistent with cooling rates on the order of $10^7\ \mathrm{K/s}$. {{cite:S3#c1}} A separate microstructural survey of SLM maraging steel found a fine-grained martensitic structure in the as-printed condition and noted an absence of pronounced texture or anisotropy across the build sections. {{cite:S8#c1}}

Heat treatment strongly modifies these as-built features: aging and solution treatments change precipitate populations, relieve residual stresses, and alter mechanical anisotropy. {{cite:S3#c1}} One study that compared nitriding, solution treatment, and aging in SLM 18Ni-300 explored how prior heat treatments influence the morphology and chemistry of a thermochemical nitride layer and showed that nitriding without prior aging can produce cracks in the compound layer, whereas nitriding after appropriate heat treatment improved wear and corrosion resistance. {{cite:S2#c1}} Heat treatments therefore interact with surface treatments and thermochemical processes to determine final microstructure and surface-layer integrity. {{cite:S2#c1}}

Austenite reversion (reversal of martensite to retained or reversed austenite) is reported to occur when maraging steel is tempered near intercritical temperatures, with reversed austenite precipitating preferentially along martensite lath boundaries where local nickel enrichment promotes nucleation. {{cite:S4#c1}} In the study that tempered SLM maraging steel at 720°C (within the α′ + γ range), the tempered specimen containing reversed austenite showed improved pitting behaviour in chloride solution: higher pitting potential, lower corrosion current density, and lower corrosion rate in 3.5 wt% NaCl than as-printed, aged, or homogenized specimens without reversed austenite. {{cite:S4#c1}}

Corrosion behaviour of SLM maraging steels is therefore controlled by a combination of intrinsic AM-induced microstructural features (fine grains, precipitates, residual stresses), by post-build heat treatments that modify precipitate state and austenite fraction, and by surface condition or thermochemical treatments such as nitriding or electropolishing. {{cite:S6#c1}}{{cite:S2#c1}} Electropolishing has been shown to substantially reduce SLM surface roughness (Ra decreased by about 65%), a factor that also affects corrosion response. {{cite:S8#c1}} Where studies directly compare corrosion resistance, heat-treated SLM parts often show corrosion performance similar to conventionally produced maraging steels when precipitate state and surface finish are comparable. {{cite:S3#c1}}{{cite:S8#c1}}

rows.bRaw:
Selective laser melting (SLM) builds metallic parts by layerwise melting and fusion of powder with a high-power laser, a process that routinely attains near-full density in suitable alloys. {{cite:S1#c1}} The unique thermal history imposed by SLM — very high local heating followed by rapid cooling — produces microstructures and properties that differ from conventional routes and can impart fine hierarchical (sub)grains and very fine precipitates in steels. {{cite:S6#c1}}

As-built SLM maraging steel frequently exhibits a very fine martensitic matrix produced by the extreme cooling rates of the process. {{cite:S3#c1}} One study reports submicron average grain sizes consistent with cooling on the order of $10^{7}\,$K/s and finds a martensitic matrix containing dense, needle-shaped Ni3X precipitates (X = Ti, Al, Mo) that contribute to age hardening. {{cite:S3#c1}} Work using EBSD across multiple build sections found that as-printed maraging steel displays a fine martensitic structure without a strong preferred texture and with similar microstructure in the xy, xz and yz planes. {{cite:S8#c1}} The as-printed surface condition can be rough (reported Ra ≈ 10 µm in one study), which is important for subsequent surface-sensitive properties. {{cite:S8#c1}}

Conventional heat treatments and thermochemical treatments modify these as-built features in distinct ways. Systematic heat-treatment studies characterized changes in precipitation, residual stress and mechanical anisotropy, and reported that suitable heat treatments both increase strength through precipitation and relieve residual stress while reducing, but not entirely removing, mechanical anisotropy. {{cite:S3#c1}} Aging and solution treatments performed prior to plasma nitriding alter the morphology and chemistry of the nitrided layer and thus influence wear and corrosion resistance; nitriding without prior aging produced cracks in the compound layer, whereas nitriding after appropriate prior heat treatment improved wear and corrosion performance. {{cite:S2#c1}} Electropolishing after SLM reduces surface roughness substantially and, together with heat treatment, affects hardness and corrosion behaviour such that nano-hardness and corrosion of heat-treated SLM material can be similar to wrought counterparts. {{cite:S8#c1}}

Reversion of martensite to austenite during intercritical tempering has been linked to improved corrosion resistance in chloride environments. {{cite:S4#c1}} In one study, tempering in the α' + γ range promoted reversed austenite formation mainly along martensite lath boundaries, driven by local Ni enrichment from dissolved intermetallics, and the specimen tempered at 720 °C showed increased pitting potential, reduced corrosion current density and a lower corrosion rate in 3.5 wt% NaCl than as-printed, aged and homogenized samples. {{cite:S4#c1}} This indicates that controlled austenite reversion can enhance pitting resistance in chloride solutions. {{cite:S4#c1}}

Agreement across studies points to (1) inherently fine, precipitation-strengthened martensitic microstructures after SLM, (2) heat treatments and thermochemical steps that can tailor precipitation, residual stress and surface layer quality, and (3) a beneficial role for reversed austenite on pitting resistance in NaCl environments. {{cite:S3#c1}}{{cite:S2#c1}}{{cite:S4#c1}}{{cite:S8#c1}}

