Household rooftop solar adoption is shaped by a mix of financial, informational, technical and institutional factors that operate together as motivators and inhibitors. Recent empirical research identifies government subsidies and financial considerations as primary motivators, while awareness, perceived technical risk and institutional barriers operate as important inhibitors. {{cite:S3#c1}}{{cite:S4#c1}}

Upfront cost and finance emerge repeatedly as direct barriers to adoption. Survey-based studies find that capital cost and expectations about maintenance costs reduce willingness to adopt, with cost-related variables entering models as negative predictors of adoption or willingness-to-adopt. {{cite:S3#c1}}{{cite:S6#c1}} Evidence from nationally representative household data in Uganda shows that affordability determines the type of solar technology adopted and that flexible payment mechanisms increase uptake among income-constrained households. {{cite:S7#c1}} These findings indicate that both the absolute upfront price and the availability of payment options affect household investment decisions. {{cite:S7#c1}}

Policy instruments—most notably subsidies—play a central role in shaping the economics of rooftop PV and thereby household decisions. Studies using behavioural and institutional frameworks identify PV subsidies as a primary motivator that reduces perceived initial investment risk and strengthens the financial case for households. {{cite:S3#c1}} National targets and policy commitments also structure deployment plans and signal government support for rooftop PV deployment. {{cite:S1#c1}}{{cite:S5#c1}} However, policy design and implementation challenges are noted as constraints on achieving stated national goals. {{cite:S1#c1}}

Awareness, knowledge and perceived technical barriers also figure prominently. Empirical models and forecasting exercises rank technical barriers and knowledge/awareness factors among significant predictors of household investment decisions in rooftop PV systems. {{cite:S3#c1}} Studies using theory-of-planned-behavior extensions report that perceived benefits, perceived risk and self-efficacy strongly influence willingness to adopt, with perceived risk and cost reducing uptake. {{cite:S6#c1}} Local pilot-project surveys that include questions about users’ experience and space availability further document experiential and informational constraints at the consumer level. {{cite:S8#c1}}

Institutional and equity considerations affect who adopts and how policy interventions perform. Research highlights demographic and spatial inequities associated with household solar adoption and argues for attention to distributional outcomes when scaling rooftop PV. {{cite:S4#c1}} Reviews of renewable-energy constraints emphasize that broader system-level obstacles—policy, regulatory and institutional capacity—hamper expansion even where resource potential exists. {{cite:S2#c1}}{{cite:S8#c1}} Together, these studies suggest that addressing upfront affordability, improving information and risk perceptions, and resolving institutional implementation gaps are central to increasing household rooftop solar uptake. {{cite:S7#c1}}{{cite:S3#c1}}

rows.bRaw:
Household decisions about rooftop photovoltaic (PV) adoption are shaped by both financial and non‑financial factors identified across studies. Upfront cost and concerns about long‑term returns act as primary financial inhibitors: research using structural equation models found that lower prices and lower maintenance costs reduce apprehension about returns, while perceived cost negatively affects willingness to adopt. {{cite:S3#c1}}{{cite:S6#c1}} Flexible payment mechanisms emerge as a practical way to address affordability constraints for income‑constrained households: evidence from Uganda shows that payment flexibility increases uptake of solar home systems and kits by enabling households to afford otherwise costly systems. {{cite:S7#c1}}

Government subsidies and policy instruments play a dual role, both motivating adoption and shaping market growth. Subsidies operate as a leading motivator that mitigates initial investment risk and strengthens the environmental and financial rationale for PV investment, according to survey‑based work in India that combines SEM and ANN analyses. {{cite:S3#c1}} National targets and policy frameworks have explicitly prioritized rooftop PV within broader solar capacity goals, embedding rooftop deployment in planning documents and pilot programmes. {{cite:S1#c1}}{{cite:S8#c1}} At the same time, the literature reviewing India’s renewable sector notes a range of obstacles that persist despite pro‑renewable policy intent, indicating gaps between policy design and on‑the‑ground uptake. {{cite:S5#c1}}

Knowledge, technical capacity, and perceived risk form important non‑financial barriers. Studies identify awareness, technical barriers, and knowledge gaps as significant predictors of household investment decisions, with technical constraints and limited consumer understanding reducing the likelihood of adoption. {{cite:S3#c1}} Perceived risk and doubts about technology effectiveness also lower willingness to adopt, as shown in multi‑city household surveys that extended the theory of planned behaviour. {{cite:S6#c1}} Pilot projects that surveyed consumers about roof space, consumption patterns, and user experience highlight practical implementation concerns that can impede deployment even where policy support exists. {{cite:S8#c1}}

Equity and spatial heterogeneity further complicate barriers to rooftop PV. Analyses of global household solar adoption identify demographic and spatial inequities—between groups and across geographies—that can lead to uneven distribution of benefits and risks as deployment scales. {{cite:S4#c1}} Empirical work in Uganda finds uptake patterns differ by residence, income, and housing type, with adoption concentrated in rural areas and driven largely by affordability; this heterogeneity implies that a single policy instrument may not address varied local constraints. {{cite:S7#c1}}

Taken together, the literature points to a constellation of interdependent barriers—high upfront cost and financing limits, the pivotal role of subsidies and policy implementation, informational and technical obstacles, and equity‑related spatial differences—that policymakers must consider when seeking to expand household rooftop solar. {{cite:S3#c1}}{{cite:S7#c1}}{{cite:S4#c1}}

