Evidence on enterprise growth and profitability is mixed but indicates that complementary inputs beyond credit can substantially increase business creation and profits. Results from a seven-year quasi-experiment in rural India show an information and communication technology (ICT) intervention produced 160 new businesses in treatment villages versus 40 in controls, and it interacted with women’s social network positions to raise entrepreneurial activity and profits. {{cite:S7#c1}} Khan et al. found that female entrepreneurs supported by microfinance are generally better off on economic indicators compared with non-entrepreneurs, supporting a link between microfinance-enabled business activity and improved economic well-being. {{cite:S1#c1}}  

Social and cultural norms shape who benefits from enterprise and how much businesses can transform women’s lives. Haugh and Talwar document that innovative business processes which complied with local social and cultural constraints nevertheless facilitated women’s economic activity and contributed to changing the social order, highlighting that entrepreneurship’s effects depend on fit with social norms. {{cite:S3#c1}} The differential impacts reported by Khan et al. — stronger economic and psychological gains but relatively smaller social and political empowerment — further indicate that business success does not automatically translate into wider shifts in women’s social standing or political voice. {{cite:S1#c1}}  

Household decision-making and relational empowerment respond differently from personal empowerment, so outcomes depend on which dimension is measured and when. Huis et al. propose a Three-Dimensional Model of Women’s Empowerment distinguishing personal (micro), relational (meso), and societal (macro) dimensions and argue that time, culture, and intervention type shape when and how these dimensions change. {{cite:S6#c1}} Khan et al.’s mixed-method evidence of limited political and social gains despite economic improvements illustrates this separation between personal economic empowerment and changes in household or community decision-making. {{cite:S1#c1}}  

Several studies warn that credit alone can be insufficient or even harmful without appropriate design and complementary support. Banerjee and Jackson’s ethnography finds that microfinance can increase indebtedness and exacerbate vulnerabilities in poor communities, calling into question claims that access to loans by itself reliably reduces poverty or strengthens women’s positions. {{cite:S5#c1}} At the institutional level, Cull et al. show that market forces and the quest for profitability shape which clients are served, suggesting that scaling microfinance alone will not fill gaps in serving the poorest without missions or subsidies targeted to those gaps. {{cite:S2#c1}}  

Synthesizing these strands, the evidence converges on three points: microfinance-facilitated entrepreneurship can expand business formation and improve individual economic outcomes in some contexts, complementary inputs such as ICT and attention to social norms amplify these effects, and relational and societal empowerment often lag personal economic gains, so credit alone is unlikely to produce broad household or community-level change. {{cite:S7#c1}}{{cite:S3#c1}}{{cite:S6#c1}}{{cite:S1#c1}}{{cite:S5#c1}}{{cite:S2#c1}}

