Evidence on microfinance-supported women’s enterprises shows consistent gains in economic and personal dimensions of empowerment, but more mixed effects on relational and societal outcomes. A quasi-experimental mixed-methods study finds that female entrepreneurs who used microfinance are better off than non-entrepreneurs on economic, social, political and psychological indicators of empowerment, although impacts are smaller for political and (to a lesser extent) social empowerment; qualitative interviews in the same study corroborated that entrepreneurship facilitates holistic empowerment when women exercise control over their ventures. {{cite:S1#c1}}

Several studies emphasize the role of non-financial factors in driving business growth and translating credit into sustained enterprise outcomes. A seven-year quasi-experiment in rural India demonstrates that women’s social networks and ICT use jointly shape both the initiation and success of ventures: ties to family and community raise ICT adoption and entrepreneurial profits, while ties to men in power have negative associations; an ICT intervention generated substantially more new businesses in treated villages than in controls. {{cite:S7#c1}} Other work on social entrepreneurship in North India argues that business processes which align with local norms can both enable women’s economic activity and contribute to changing the social order, suggesting that culturally attuned business models matter for scale and sustainability. {{cite:S3#c1}} Studies of women owner-managers in tourism likewise show how entrepreneurs blend social-transformational and commercial goals, indicating sector- and strategy-specific pathways beyond mere credit. {{cite:S8#c1}}

The literature also highlights limits of credit alone and potential harms when market pressures dominate. Ethnographic research in Bangladeshi villages documents cases where microfinance increased indebtedness and exacerbated economic and social vulnerabilities, and where market-based interventions undermined social capital that might otherwise support entrepreneurial capabilities. {{cite:S5#c1}} At the institutional level, reviews of the microfinance industry note that commercialization and for-profit trajectories change who is served and how subsidies and social missions influence reach; profitability pressures can leave the poorest underserved, implying that credit provision without supportive social objectives may not achieve inclusive enterprise growth. {{cite:S2#c1}} {{cite:S4#c1}}

A synthesis of empowerment findings proposes a Three-Dimensional Model that helps explain heterogeneous results: empowerment can occur on the micro (personal), meso (relational) and macro (societal) levels, and the timing and cultural context affect which dimensions materialize after interventions. {{cite:S6#c1}} This model clarifies why studies find strong personal and economic gains from entrepreneurship but weaker or delayed effects on household decision-making, community status and political influence—outcomes that depend on relational dynamics, social norms and longer time horizons rather than on credit alone. {{cite:S6#c1}}

