Household-level studies converge on a multi-dimensional friction model in which informational, procedural, structural and financial barriers interact to block adoption between awareness and installation. Karnataka-focused qualitative evidence reports that adoption remains constrained by informational gaps, procedural complexity, structural limitations, and perceived financial risk despite a progressive policy framework. {{cite:S1#c2}} Bagla’s exploratory fieldwork in Karnataka used semi-structured interviews and questionnaires with 70 households (24 adopters, 46 non-adopters) to trace how these frictions operate along the adoption journey; responses were analysed thematically and triangulated with national surveys and a Kerala qualitative study. {{cite:S1#c1}} The study finds that anticipated procedural complexity exceeds experienced complexity among adopters, who revise expected difficulty downward after installation, and that space and roof-ownership constraints, information gaps and upfront cost rank highly among non-adopters. {{cite:S1#c1}}

Procedural frictions show up both as real administrative burdens and as perception-driven deterrents. Bagla documents a “subsidy paradox” in which restrictions on panels, vendor empanelment and paperwork dilute the subsidy’s perceived value for some households. {{cite:S1#c1}} This aligns with broader findings that uptake depends on how households perceive complexity and observability: diffusion-of-innovation analysis across India found that perceived relative advantage, compatibility, reduced complexity and observability positively influence purchase intentions. {{cite:S4#c1}} Where complexity is high or visible, prospective adopters delay or forgo installation even when technical potential and subsidies exist. {{cite:S1#c1}}{{cite:S4#c1}}

Financial and reliability concerns remain salient in rural and small-town contexts. A case study of rural households found that while many perceive solar PV as cheap and environmentally friendly, there is widespread concern that solar delivers inferior quality energy, is vulnerable to theft, and requires high capital investment beyond many households’ means; the authors therefore call for financing-sensitive rollout designs and robust awareness campaigns on reliability and cost-effectiveness. {{cite:S2#c1}} Quantitative work using the Theory of Planned Behaviour in North Kerala reports that government subsidy awareness and cost–benefit perception positively affect attitude toward installation, and that attitude, subjective norms and perceived behavioural control significantly influence intention to install. {{cite:S6#c1}}

Contextual variation matters: city-level surveys emphasise governance and multisector collaboration for public participation, arguing that barriers in Patna resemble those elsewhere but require locally tailored information-system and institutional responses. {{cite:S3#c1}} Household perception surveys from Meerut similarly highlight cost, environmental concern, incentives and trust as determinants of adoption, reinforcing the cross-regional pattern that informational clarity and trustworthiness of institutions and vendors shape uptake. {{cite:S5#c1}}

Collectively, the literature paints a picture in which subsidies alone do not guarantee adoption: procedural design, information provision, finance mechanisms and structural constraints on roofs jointly determine whether households move from awareness to installation. {{cite:S1#c1}}{{cite:S2#c1}}{{cite:S6#c1}}

Household-level studies identify four recurring frictions—informational gaps, procedural complexity, structural constraints, and perceived financial risk—that interrupt the pathway from awareness to installation. Evidence from Karnataka, based on semi-structured interviews with 70 households, directly attributes stalled residential adoption to these four frictions despite an enabling policy environment and capital subsidies. {{cite:S1#c2}} The Karnataka study used thematic analysis of questionnaires and interviews with 24 adopters and 46 non-adopters in the Bengaluru region, and triangised its findings with a large pan-India survey and state-level qualitative work. {{cite:S1#c1}}

Information frictions appear in perceptions of technology reliability and in awareness of subsidies and procedures. In rural case studies, households perceive solar as relatively cheap and environmentally friendly but also express doubts about energy quality, theft risk, and high capital needs, suggesting that favourable attitudes coexist with substantive informational and trust deficits. {{cite:S2#c1}} Quantitative analyses find that awareness of government subsidies predicts positive attitudes toward rooftop systems, and attitude, subjective norms and perceived behavioural control predict installation intention, with the structural model explaining a modest share of variance in intention. {{cite:S6#c1}}

Procedural friction emerges from expectations and experiences of complexity and from administrative restrictions attached to subsidies. In Karnataka, prospective adopters overestimated installation complexity: expected complexity averaged $5.1$ out of $10$, while adopters reported actual complexity at $2.2$, a $57$ per cent reduction, indicating that anticipated procedural barriers can deter action more than experienced barriers justify. {{cite:S1#c1}} The Karnataka study also documents a “subsidy paradox” where vendor empanelment, panel specifications and paperwork dilute subsidy value, linking procedural requirements directly to the perceived financial benefit. {{cite:S1#c1}}

Structural constraints—principally roof space and ownership—rank highly among non-adopters in Karnataka and dominate the stated barriers ahead of information and upfront cost. {{cite:S1#c1}} Broad cross-sectional work applying diffusion-of-innovation dimensions finds that perceived complexity, relative advantage, compatibility and observability influence purchase intentions, implying that physical compatibility and observability of systems are important determinants across contexts. {{cite:S4#c1}}

Financial frictions combine upfront capital concerns with perceptions of low long-run value; rural households report that solar requires high capital beyond reach, even where they view operating costs as low. {{cite:S2#c1}} In Karnataka, nearly half of adopters reported they would have adopted even without the subsidy, suggesting heterogeneity in subsidy dependence and pointing to perception- and procedure-driven attenuation of subsidy effectiveness. {{cite:S1#c1}}

Across studies, methods vary from small qualitative case studies and regionally targeted surveys to large purposive samples and structural equation models, yet they consistently surface overlapping barriers of information, procedures, structure and finance, while differing in emphasis and explanatory power across urban and rural settings. {{cite:S3#c1}}{{cite:S4#c1}} [[NEEDS SOURCE: detailed evidence on subsidy take-up rates by income]]

