Despite Karnataka’s comparatively advanced distributed-solar policy framework, household uptake remains well below technical potential because adoption is blocked by a bundle of frictions at the household level: information shortfalls, procedural complexity, structural limits on roofs and perceived financial risk. {{cite:S1#c2}} Evidence from a qualitative study of 70 Karnataka households finds these same four friction types concentrated in the “last mile” between policy and installation, with non-adopters most frequently citing limited roof space and ambiguous roof ownership as the principal constraint. {{cite:S1#c1}}

That Karnataka study used semi-structured interviews and questionnaires to compare adopters and non-adopters and to measure expectations against experience. {{cite:S1#c1}} It reports a striking gap between anticipated and experienced procedural burden: prospective adopters expected a mean complexity score of $5.1$ out of $10$ before installation but recorded an actual post-installation score of $2.2$, a 57 per cent reduction in perceived complexity. {{cite:S1#c1}} This mismatch underlines two related frictions: overestimated procedural difficulty that deters households prematurely, and post-installation narratives that sometimes fail to propagate to prospective buyers.

Financial perceptions and the way subsidies are delivered create additional obstacles. The Karnataka sample shows a mixed role for subsidies: nearly half of adopters (45.8 per cent) said they would have adopted even without the capital subsidy, while others described a “subsidy paradox” in which restrictions on panel types, vendor empanelment rules and paperwork reduce the practical value of the subsidy. {{cite:S1#c1}} Broader studies similarly document that households regard solar as capital-intensive and sometimes unaffordable, which amplifies perceived financial risk. {{cite:S2#c1}}

Information and trust deficits recur across contexts. Surveys and factor-analytic work from other Indian cities identify complexity, observability and compatibility as central determinants of purchase intention, so that households’ beliefs about relative advantage and reliability shape uptake. {{cite:S4#c1}} Studies in Patna and Meerut report that gaps in public awareness, limited coordination among agencies and doubts about technological reliability or service quality act as persistent barriers to wider adoption. {{cite:S3#c1}}{{cite:S5#c1}}

Behavioural models corroborate these mechanisms: a TPB-based study in Kerala finds that environmental concern, awareness of government subsidies and cost–benefit perceptions strengthen pro-solar attitudes, while attitude, subjective norms and perceived behavioural control together explain a nontrivial share of intention variance. {{cite:S6#c1}} Taken together, the literature converges on a multi-causal account in which structural constraints (roof space/ownership), distorted expectations about procedures, subsidy design and persistent information/trust gaps jointly slow household rooftop solar adoption. {{cite:S1#c1}}{{cite:S4#c1}}
